      "writtenOn": "2024-08-01T13:07:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Are there plans to do this for the other BindGroup types? For example there is a cache for uniform buffer bind groups on the ResourceProvider. This seems like it would have all the same memory issues. So if we thing having Texture hold \"refs\" to its BindGroups that it is part of, then I think we should also have all Buffers hold refs to BindGroups as well. If this was simply about being more efficient in cache look ups, then sure just having this on Texture is fine. But the main driver for this change is to not be leaking GPU memory. So I think the system here needs to be expanded to a more general model that includes any resource that may be held in a BindGroup.",
      "revId": "f6d3f83e407a738f7eba91e3be06f3b74de638ad",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f951fc27_3619c75a",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5036
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-08-01T13:10:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Another alternative to all this is we remove the BindGroup caching from Graphite entirely and instead have Dawn cache them. Is there something that is blocking Dawn from just have a cache of them it can returning one when you ask to create a new one?",
      "parentUuid": "0953c0f6_0afabbb2",
      "revId": "f6d3f83e407a738f7eba91e3be06f3b74de638ad",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9abba0aa_3a584e3f",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 19434
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-08-01T13:24:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Buffers BindGroups contain multiple buffers, it\u0027s more complicated to clear than the texture BindGroups (specifically handled in this CL) which contain only one texture. Furthermore, we don\u0027t have external buffers like textures (i.e. `BackendTexture`). So we don\u0027t need to clear the buffers\u0027 BindGroups to avoid memory leaks.\n\nFor textures, since currently we only cache single texture\u0027s BindGroups and it\u0027s 90% of the usecases, I think it\u0027s valid to move the texture cache to be owned by DawnTexture.\n\nDawn currently doesn\u0027t cache the BindGroups. Unlike Dawn, Graphite has full knowledge about how many buffers, textures should be in a BindGroup, hence IMO it\u0027s much more easier and faster to cache in Graphite because of static sized keys.",
      "parentUuid": "f951fc27_3619c75a",
      "revId": "f6d3f83e407a738f7eba91e3be06f3b74de638ad",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ee3bcc33_54253423",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5036
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-08-01T13:31:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That makes sense about why it is probably better to cache in Graphite instead of Dawn.\n\nI\u0027m confused why textures being external impacts whether memory leaks or not. Like today if we drop all our refs to a Buffer, but we\u0027re still keeping a BindGroup that references that Buffer in our cache, we\u0027ll never clean up the memory right? Or is the idea that we\u0027ll continue to call Delete on Buffer objects (so bind group holding refs should matter), but want to stop calling Delete for Textures because the last usage of a Texture might be external to Skia and we don\u0027t want to force clients to use dawns Delete methods if they don\u0027t want to?",
      "parentUuid": "9abba0aa_3a584e3f",
      "revId": "f6d3f83e407a738f7eba91e3be06f3b74de638ad",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a708733b_44ad9ca5",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 19434
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-08-01T13:53:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "TLDR, yes, I think we can still use `Destroy` on buffers because it\u0027s ok to do so. But for textures, we don\u0027t want to use that anymore because it prevents `submit` calls from being deferred.\n\nSince buffers are entirely controlled by Graphite. When we call `Buffer::freeGpuResources`, we know that this is the last reference of the buffer, so we can use `Destroy()` safely.\n\nHowever, which external texture, when `Texture::freeGpuResources` is called, it is not safe to call `Destroy()` because the texture is owned externally. So now we have two different places that control the lifetime of the texture. I think it\u0027s complicated to guarantee the leak free across all places. We have to make sure that client calls `Destroy` properly on the textures.\n\nWith the change in this CL, everything will be in Graphite\u0027s control. Whether client calls `texture.Destroy` or not, there won\u0027t be any leak.\n\nThe second reason is that this is for Chrome\u0027s shared images (SI) system. For SI, only textures are relevant, and we don\u0027t want to call `Destroy` on them, because we want to be able to defer the `Context::submit` to the end of a frame, past the lifetime of the SI. As of now, we have to call `Destroy` when a SI is deleted, and it\u0027s not valid to call `Context::submit` that has pending commands using the texture after that.\n\nFWIW, I think we can also use a general approach of making buffers and textures BindGroups follow the same mechanism as you suggested:\n- Cleaning up BindGroups properly. Holding BG refs in textures and buffers.\n- Don\u0027t call `Destroy` on any buffers or textures.\n\nBut IMO it\u0027s more complicated, and not necessary at this point, because calling `Destroy` on buffers is not a problem so we don\u0027t have to fix it. Also as you mentioned, for single texture BG, caching in DawnTexture indeed has faster lookups.",
      "parentUuid": "ee3bcc33_54253423",
      "revId": "f6d3f83e407a738f7eba91e3be06f3b74de638ad",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "889c275c_f2336c45",
